WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.450
Ötzi: The Frozen Man from the Alps
On September 19, 1991, a couple of tourists

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:08.849
were hiking through the alpine mountains that
straddle the border between Austria and Italy

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:14.429
when they came upon a grisly sight - a frozen
body with the lower half completely encased

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:15.429
in ice.

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:19.170
They initially thought they discovered the
remains of an unfortunate mountaineer who

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.039
maybe got lost or injured and died in the
snow.

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:26.520
Or perhaps they stumbled onto something a
bit more blood-curdling like a murder victim.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.529
Either way, they did what any sensible person
would do and alerted the authorities.

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:35.650
As it turned out, their suspicions were off
by a few thousand years.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.270
A murder victim?

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:42.030
Maybe (more on that later), but definitely
not a recently-deceased mountaineer.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:45.850
The body belonged to a man from the Chalcolithic,
better known as the Copper Age.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.830
He has often been referred to as Ötzi the
Iceman, named after the Ötztal Alps where

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:56.320
he was found, and became somewhat of a sensation
with him being the oldest natural mummy in

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.320
Europe.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.640
Today we are doing something a bit different
here at Biographics as we explore the story

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.570
of a man completely absent from the historical
record.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:10.080
There are no texts, no inscriptions, no oral
history to provide us with information about

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.220
his family life, his accomplishments, or his
failures.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.810
Every single thing we know about Ötzi, we
learned by studying his mummified body and

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:24.680
the items he had on him when he died.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:33.159
The Discovery

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:38.240
As we mentioned above, the iceman was discovered
by two German hikers named Helmut and Erika

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.840
Simon on the ridge of the Fineil peak, at
an elevation of 10,530 feet.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.799
The two tourists were venturing off the beaten
path when they encountered Ötzi’s half-thawed

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:53.320
body so it is hard to say with certainty when
he first emerged from his icy tomb.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:54.320
Most likely, it was that year’s warm summer
that caused enough ice to melt to allow the

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.320
head and torso to protrude out.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.119
Extracting the mummy from the snow proved
trickier than expected.

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:00.330
The first day, the Simons found the body but
made no attempt to remove it.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:04.260
The second day, they brought a few policemen
who came armed with pickaxes and a pneumatic

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.010
drill, but they had to abandon the mission
due to bad weather.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:10.569
The third day, another attempt was aborted
because they could not obtain a helicopter.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:16.370
It wasn’t until September 23 that Ötzi
was completely extricated from the ice and

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.700
taken to the Institute of Forensic Medicine
in Innsbruck.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.739
There, archaeologist Konrad Spindler dismissed
notions that the body belonged to a recently-deceased

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:29.560
mountaineer and said that it was 4,000 years
old, at least.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.120
Scientists realized the potential magnitude
of the find and multiple teams went back to

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.040
the location where Ötzi was buried and continued
excavations to see what else they could discover.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.410
Meanwhile, four separate scientific institutions
were given tissue samples from the corpse

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:48.069
to perform radiocarbon dating and they all
concluded that the ice mummy lived sometime

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:52.269
between 3350 and 3100 BC.

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:57.279
To put that into some perspective, Ötzi had
already been buried for over 600 years when

00:02:57.279 --> 00:03:01.019
the ancient Egyptians began construction on
the Great Pyramid of Giza.

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:06.139
In the meantime, modern authorities were more
concerned over the complicated issue of ownership.

00:03:06.139 --> 00:03:10.870
With little regard for future geopolitical
sensibilities, Ötzi decided to die right

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:15.109
next to a somewhat-controversial border between
Austria and Italy.

00:03:15.109 --> 00:03:20.329
According to the Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye
from 1919, the border between North Tyrol

00:03:20.329 --> 00:03:25.859
(which is in Austria) and South Tyrol (which
is in Italy) is defined as the drainage divide

00:03:25.859 --> 00:03:28.019
of the rivers Inn and Etsch.

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:32.249
However, when it was time to officially draw
the borders on maps, the survey team made

00:03:32.249 --> 00:03:35.449
a mistake due to heavy snow and placed the
border incorrectly.

00:03:35.449 --> 00:03:40.099
As a result, Ötzi was dug up by Austrian
authorities and taken to an Austrian institute

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:41.099
for study.

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:46.690
However, two weeks after his discovery, on
October 2, 1991, a new survey was carried

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:52.540
out which determined that the mummy had actually
been located about 300 feet from the border,

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.640
but on the Italian side.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.909
Therefore, South Tyrol successfully claimed
property rights, but agreed to let the scientific

00:03:58.909 --> 00:04:02.340
examination of the iceman be carried out in
Austria.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:07.909
Consequently, Ötzi has been at the South
Tyrol Museum of Archaeology in Italy for over

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:10.519
two decades.

00:04:10.519 --> 00:04:17.579
What We Learned from the Body
Ötzi, or the Similaun Man as he is sometimes

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:20.229
called, is categorized as a wet mummy.

00:04:20.229 --> 00:04:25.120
This means that its body was dehydrated and
lost most of its fluid, but the tissue and

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.470
organs are still well-preserved.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:31.470
Ötzi’s corpse has become one of the most
extensively analyzed and examined human remains

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.550
in history and, believe it or not, we are
still learning new stuff about him and the

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:36.550
age he lived in.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.919
We are fortunate that he was preserved almost
in his entirety because he ended up in a crevasse

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:45.450
that shielded him from the harshest weather
elements, but also perfectly encased him inside

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:49.650
a glacier and protected him from being crushed
during the glacier’s movements.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:54.199
As a result of this, we got an amazing glimpse
into the life, the behavior, the clothing,

00:04:54.199 --> 00:04:58.729
the tools, and even the diet of primitive
Europeans from over 5,000 years ago.

00:04:58.729 --> 00:05:01.139
Ötzi was a relatively small man for the time.

00:05:01.139 --> 00:05:04.419
His mummy is a little over 5 ft tall and weighs
close to 29 lbs.

00:05:04.419 --> 00:05:09.259
In life, he would have been a bit taller,
something like 5 ft 2 in, and would have weighed

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:10.470
around 110 lbs.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:14.990
By examining structural bone units called
osteons from the mummy’s femur, researchers

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:18.229
estimated that Ötzi was around 45 years old
when he died.

00:05:18.229 --> 00:05:22.990
Most of his hair was gone because the epidermis
quickly shed during the process of decomposition.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:28.130
However, a few clumps were still present around
the remains, indicating that Ötzi had dark,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.599
medium-long hair with traces of arsenic in
it.

00:05:30.599 --> 00:05:35.190
That last part suggests to researchers that
the iceman was involved in smelting, most

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:39.139
likely copper since he had an axe blade that
was almost pure copper.

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:42.699
This belief was further strengthened by the
fact that Ötzi’s lungs were blackened by

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:46.210
soot due to close, prolonged exposure to open
fires.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:50.350
Australian scientists from Canberra examined
the isotopes in Ötzi’s teeth and bones

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:52.479
to see what information they could glean.

00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:57.569
By studying the minerals present in his body,
researchers extrapolated the composition of

00:05:57.569 --> 00:06:01.550
his drinking water and, consequently, identified
the places where he lived.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:06.430
They believe they pinpointed his origins fairly
accurately and, unsurprisingly, they are not

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:08.439
far from the site where he was discovered.

00:06:08.439 --> 00:06:12.650
They ascertained that the iceman lived somewhere
in the valleys within 37 miles south of his

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:13.680
icy grave.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.830
Moreover, they detected contrasts in the isotopic
compositions of his tooth enamel which suggested

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:23.689
that, at one point, he left his childhood
home behind and moved around as an adult.

00:06:23.689 --> 00:06:28.919
The rest of his bones suggested that the Similaun
Man led a tough, physically-active lifestyle.

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:33.510
He had broken several bones during his lifetime
and x-rays revealed that his shoulders, knees,

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:37.479
hips, and spine all had significant wear and
tear in the joints.

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:41.610
One study indicated that the iceman’s tibia
showed mediolateral strengthening well above

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:46.439
the average for late Neolithic men, something
that could have been indicative of long walks

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:47.500
over rough terrain.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:52.139
This, compounded with the rest of the physical
stress exerted on his bones, led to speculation

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:56.229
that Ötzi may have been a high-altitude shepherd.

00:06:56.229 --> 00:07:01.469
How Healthy Was He?

00:07:01.469 --> 00:07:05.810
This should not come as much of a surprise
to you, but Ötzi was not a particularly healthy

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:08.099
individual, at least not by our standards.

00:07:08.099 --> 00:07:12.229
In fact, he currently holds the distinction
for oldest evidence of Lyme disease, an infectious

00:07:12.229 --> 00:07:17.520
illness which is transmitted to humans by
the Borrelia bacterium spread by ticks.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.569
Scientists could not tell how advanced the
infection was, but it could have given the

00:07:20.569 --> 00:07:22.999
iceman joint pains and severe headaches.

00:07:22.999 --> 00:07:27.599
In addition to his bacterial companion, Ötzi
was also infested with Trichuris trichiura,

00:07:27.599 --> 00:07:30.710
a parasite better known as whipworm.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:35.150
Eggs belonging to the parasite were still
located in the iceman’s intestinal tract.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:40.330
Perhaps the most curious medical affliction
that befell Ötzi was severe arteriosclerosis.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:44.960
This is a hardening of the arteries which
scientists previously regarded as a problem

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.249
of the modern world.

00:07:46.249 --> 00:07:51.279
Specifically, it is usually an issue for people
who follow the couch potato/fast food lifestyle

00:07:51.279 --> 00:07:56.509
as being overweight and getting little exercise
were considered the major risk factors, alongside

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:57.590
smoking and drinking.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:03.689
And yet Ötzi feasted on no fast food and
reposed on no recliner, but he was still a

00:08:03.689 --> 00:08:05.020
heart attack waiting to happen.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:09.860
As it turned out, he had a genetic predisposition
for arteriosclerosis, something which plays

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.830
a bigger part in the condition than we previously
expected.

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:17.469
Ötzi was also lactose intolerant, but this
was still the norm amongst Late Neolithic

00:08:17.469 --> 00:08:18.469
Europeans.

00:08:18.469 --> 00:08:23.129
Dairy products represented a relatively new
food group and their systems were still learning

00:08:23.129 --> 00:08:24.199
to process them.

00:08:24.199 --> 00:08:28.529
In addition to all these, the iceman also
had a few peculiarities that weren’t necessarily

00:08:28.529 --> 00:08:30.009
bad, just unusual.

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:35.500
He only had 11 pairs of ribs instead of 12,
he had an abnormally thick bony ridge above

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.600
his eyes and lacked wisdom teeth.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.380
The rest of his teeth, you won’t be surprised
to learn, were full of cavities and worn down,

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.320
most likely due to his high-carb, grain-heavy
diet.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:50.260
So, all in all, Ötzi wasn’t exactly the
picture of health.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:57.220
The Last Meal
Speaking of his diet, the iceman’s remains

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:01.420
were so well-preserved that they still contained
Ötzi’s last meal.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:06.380
It seems that just hours before he died, the
Similaun Man gorged on a high-fat feast that

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.870
would put most fast food menus to shame.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:12.720
We don’t know whether this was a regular
occurrence for Ötzi or whether he realized

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:16.900
he was dying and decided there was no point
in letting good food go to waste.

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:19.220
Most of the meal consisted of fatty ibex meat.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:23.010
In its condition, it still had striations
on the meat fibers which were a sign that

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:26.560
it had been air-dried or cooked at low temperatures
to last longer.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:30.761
Modern tests indicate that boiling the meat
or roasting it or preparing it in any way

00:09:30.761 --> 00:09:35.600
using temperatures over 60 degrees Celsius
or 140 degrees Fahrenheit would cause that

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.650
striated pattern to disappear.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.980
Ötzi complemented his ibex meat with a side
of red deer.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.900
We’re not sure what part, though.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.520
While the ibex meat was clearly fatty muscle,
the red deer meat he ate was, most likely,

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.700
an organ like the spleen or liver.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.700
His stomach also contained traces of einkorn
wheat.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:56.810
Most curiously, it appears that the iceman
also feasted on a type of fern called bracken

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:59.870
which is mainly notable for being toxic.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:03.760
Researchers are still a bit puzzled over this
one and haven’t found a conclusive explanation

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:04.760
yet.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:08.170
Was it simply a mistake and Ötzi thought
it was something else?

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:12.590
Did he use it to wrap his meat and accidentally
ingested a few pieces?

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:16.920
Did he actually use it as medicine to treat
a stomach ache?

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:21.690
Or, again, maybe he realized he was dying
and just thought “Might as well.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:24.630
Things can’t get any worse.”

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:31.010
The Tattoos
An interesting aspect about the Similaun Man’s

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.440
body was that it was covered in tattoos.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.680
Originally, scientists spotted somewhere around
40, but that number steadily increased as

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.270
they continued to inspect the remains closely
and found new ways of contrasting the black

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.120
tattoos against the dark, leathery brown skin.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.630
As of the most recent mapping of Ötzi’s
body which took place in 2015, researchers

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:53.130
spotted 61 tattoos grouped into 19 different
sections.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:57.240
The tattoos themselves are very basic and
consist of black lines arranged either in

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.170
cruciform patterns or parallel to each other.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:04.430
They were probably made by cutting small incisions
into the skin and rubbing in charcoal.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:09.351
Admittedly, the tattoos didn’t exactly turn
Ötzi into the Illustrated Man, but scientists

00:11:09.351 --> 00:11:12.950
are more interested in their purpose rather
than their quality.

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:17.730
Were they purely for decoration, did they
have some kind of symbolic or ceremonial meaning

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:19.700
or was it possible, perhaps, that their purpose
was medicinal?

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:24.290
That last option has really intrigued researchers
ever since they noticed that all of the tattoos

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:29.860
are grouped into clusters and placed on Ötzi’s
lower back, wrists, ankles, and knees - all

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:33.820
places that suffered degeneration and likely
to cause pain.

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:37.950
Perhaps they were used as an ancient pain
relief treatment similar to acupuncture, only

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.000
thousands of years before the practice was
used in China.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.720
Vintage Chalcolithic
We’ve covered about all there is to say

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:45.870
about Ötzi based on his remains, but there
is plenty more to discuss.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:51.080
When the iceman was first discovered, he was
decked out in a full assortment of clothing,

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.020
giving us a glimpse into Late Neolithic fashion.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:58.080
Ötzi was a man who liked his leather and
he used skins and hides from at least five

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:00.140
different animals to craft his outfit.

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:04.500
One of the researchers who helped analyze
and recreate his clothing called him “pretty

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:09.580
picky” when it came to selecting his materials
so it seems that Ötzi may be the earliest-known

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.560
fashionista in history.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:16.640
The iceman was wearing a coat, leggings, a
loincloth, a cloak, a belt, a cap, and shoes.

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:20.800
His loincloth was sheepskin, his leggings
were made out of goat hide and his coat was

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:25.060
a mixture of both goat and sheep hides that
had been stitched together with animal sinew.

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:29.980
The coat had been in use for a long time as
it had signs of makeshift repairs using grass

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:30.980
fibers.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:35.600
His cloak was made out of woven alpine swamp
grass and his hat was a fancy bearskin cap

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:38.690
that even had a chinstrap to keep it in place.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:42.500
Whether the cloak was actually a cloak or
not is still a matter of debate as too little

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:45.080
of it survived to get an idea of its full
size and shape.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:49.570
Some have argued that it could have been part
of a backpack or simply a mat that the iceman

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:51.131
unfurled and slept on.

00:12:51.131 --> 00:12:55.070
The belt was made out of calfskin and had
another leather strip stitched onto it to

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:59.830
make a small pouch which contained several
items that we will talk about in a minute.

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:04.300
Ötzi also carried a quiver manufactured from
the leather of a roe deer.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.750
An impressive part of the iceman’s outfit
were the shoes . They were made out of two

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:09.400
layers stitched together.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:14.280
The inner layer consisted of insulating dry
grass stuffed into a netting made out of bast

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:15.280
fiber string.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:18.160
The outer layer was deer hide, worn with the
fur on the inside.

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:22.670
A Czech footwear expert who specialized in
recreating ancient shoes created replicas

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:27.350
of Ötzi’s shoes and gave them to experienced
climbers to use them on mountain terrain.

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:30.780
They found that the footwear was not only
comfortable, but offered great protection

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:34.460
and the researcher even got an offer from
a Czech company that wanted to buy the rights

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:37.640
to sell the shoes.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.830
What’s In The Bag?

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:45.221
To complement his snazzy outfit, Ötzi was
also completely kitted out with ancient gear.

00:13:45.221 --> 00:13:50.420
The highlight of his equipment was a copper
axe which is still, to this day, a one-of-a-kind

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.420
discovery.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:56.030
The haft was approximately 24 inches long,
it was made from yew and had a right-angled

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:57.930
crook at one end to hold in the blade.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:03.410
That blade was 99.7 percent pure copper, it
was fixed into the shaft of the handle with

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:06.400
birch tar and tied together with leather straps.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:10.830
This tool saw a lot of action as the blade
showed signs of being re-sharpened numerous

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:11.830
times.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:15.470
This was clearly a valuable item and similar
axes from around that time period had been

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:17.550
found buried with high-ranking men.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.260
This led to speculation about Ötzi’s origins.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:23.220
Maybe he had not been just a shepherd or a
smelter.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:27.300
Maybe he was someone of high status like a
tribe chief.

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:30.320
Scientists actually managed to trace the source
of the copper and found that it came from

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:34.850
southern Tuscany, hundreds of miles from where
the iceman was discovered, even though the

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:37.930
alpine region had its own copper ore sources.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:42.220
Instead of providing answers, these finds
only seemed to deepen the mystery surrounding

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:43.330
the Similaun Man.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:46.000
There was plenty more equipment in the iceman’s
toolbag.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.840
We already mentioned that he had a quiver
and it seems he was in the process of assembling

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:51.070
a full bow &amp; arrow set.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:55.420
He had a long stave made from yew which he
was carving into a functioning bow.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:15:00.190
He also had 14 arrows made from wayfaring
tree and cornelian cherry, although only two

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:03.290
of them had been finished with arrowheads
and fletching.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:07.530
The quiver also contained a few antler tips
and a long piece of bast fiber which would

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.840
have probably served as the bowstring.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:14.400
Although not as impressive as the copper axe,
but just as unique were Ötzi’s dagger and

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:15.400
retoucher.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:20.210
The dagger had a flint blade, an ash wood
handle, and a wicker sheath and it still is

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:23.070
the only fully-preserved dagger from the Copper
Age.

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.740
The retoucher was a tool that puzzled researchers
for a while as they couldn’t figure out

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:27.740
its purpose.

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:31.920
It looked like a large pencil made from the
branch of a lime tree and with a sharpened

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:35.740
“core” inside that turned out to be a
piece of fire-hardened antler.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:39.780
They eventually realized that the retoucher
was used to re-sharpen other tools and weapons,

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.520
another first for that time period.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.570
Ötzi also had on him two birch-bark containers
which held charcoal fragments and various

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:47.570
leaves.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:50.920
Given that the insides of one container was
blackened, researchers speculate that the

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:54.080
iceman used them to carry embers around to
start fires.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:58.050
The rest of the gear included a few pieces
of wooden rods and planks which were once

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:03.120
part of a backpack, a bird belt used to carry
fowl, and two pieces of birch fungus which

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.670
were threaded onto leather strips, most likely
used as medicine.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:14.840
The Coldest Case
Now we arrive at, perhaps, the biggest puzzle

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:18.040
that still surrounds Ötzi - what caused his
death?

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:19.440
Was he caught in a blizzard?

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:23.820
Did he injure himself, thus becoming unable
to return to his tribe before freezing to

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:24.820
death?

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:26.310
Was he a ritual sacrifice?

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.300
Or was there foul play involved?

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:32.910
For a decade after his discovery, scientists
generally believed that Ötzi died of exposure

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:33.910
to the elements.

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:37.770
It wasn’t until years later that a more
advanced CT scan of his remains revealed that

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:40.700
the iceman had an arrow wound beneath his
left collarbone.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:44.560
Moreover, the shot hit an artery and caused
massive blood loss which would have caused

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:47.790
Ötzi to go into shock and possibly suffer
a heart attack.

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:51.740
Even today, the chances of surviving such
an attack are around 40 percent, so the iceman

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:55.180
had virtually no way of making it out alive.

00:16:55.180 --> 00:16:58.430
Investigating his death as a regular cold
case, detectives concluded that Ötzi’s

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:00.200
assailant got away with murder.

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.390
Signs pointed to an ambush.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:05.230
Just hours before his death, the iceman enjoyed
a heavy meal so he wasn’t in a rush.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:09.300
There were no defense wounds on his body except
a cut on his right hand which was a few days

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.130
old and had already begun to heal at the time
of death.

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:16.041
As far as motivation goes, investigators ruled
out profit because the killer didn’t take

00:17:16.041 --> 00:17:17.740
Ötzi’s prized copper axe.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:22.680
Despite their conclusions, detectives reluctantly
admit that this is one cold case destined

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:25.300
to remain unsolved forever.

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:28.319
Someone may have taken out the iceman, but
they didn’t take out his bloodline.

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:31.620
Believe it or not, Ötzi has descendants still
alive today.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:36.750
When scientists sequenced his genome back
in 2013, they compared a unique genetic mutation

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:41.390
to thousands of samples obtained from donors
in Tyrol and found that 19 of them shared

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:43.130
the same ancestry as Ötzi.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:47.370
They were all men, as it appears only the
paternal line survived.

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:51.630
Who Owns Ötzi?

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:55.020
Controversy seems to have followed Ötzi even
in death.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.840
We already mentioned that there was a little
tiff between Austria and Italy over who owned

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:03.220
the remains, but that matter was solved quickly
and pales in comparison to the legal dispute

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:06.910
between the original discoverers and the Government
of South Tyrol.

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:10.830
According to Italian law, Helmut and Erika
Simon were entitled to a finder’s fee for

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:14.260
locating the iceman equal to 25 percent of
his total value.

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:17.910
However, the provincial government simply
refused to pay this, arguing that they are

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:21.680
the ones who covered the expenses of removing
and preserving the iceman.

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:25.580
In 1994, they offered a measly reward of 10
million lire (€5,200).

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.720
Unsurprisingly, the Simons refused and went
to court.

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:32.870
It wasn’t until 2008, 17 years later, that
the matter was settled.

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:36.940
Although Helmut Simon had died by then, his
widow received 150,000 euros.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:42.340
Simon, who died during a freak blizzard while
hiking not far from where the iceman was found,

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:46.960
was just one of several bizarre deaths linked
to Ötzi, prompting rumors of a curse similar

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:48.400
to the one of the pharaohs.

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:52.310
Of course, given that hundreds of people were
involved in the discovery and study of the

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:56.420
frozen man, having a few of them die over
the course of decades isn’t that unusual.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:19:01.300
Even so, the “Curse of the Iceman” just
helps add to the mystique that still surrounds

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:23.650
Ötzi.

